# Linux_CharDevice_Driver
This repository showcases practical demo implementations of **Linux generic character device drivers**.

The demo collection covers multiple common kernel subsystems built upon the character device framework, including the platform LED driver, input subsystem, IIO subsystem, I2C subsystem and more.
These drivers may adopt different implementation ideas referenced from various kernel source code segments, and **the demo codes may target different Linux kernel versions (not limited to Linux 4.1.15)**, but all are strictly compliant with official Linux kernel programming standards.

Each independent driver directory contains its own dedicated README document.
The separate README explains implementation details, design ideas, and also points out existing limitations and defects of the demo code.
Great efforts are made to pursue code reusability and support driver multi-instantiation for every example.
All demos feature complete error handling logic and layered resource rollback to avoid memory leaks.

## Features
- Code written in compliance with official Linux kernel coding standards
- Demos support different Linux kernel versions, not limited to a single kernel version
- Strive for high code reusability and multi-instantiation capability
- Layered error rollback and complete resource release to prevent memory leaks
- Device tree hardware description adopted in relevant demos
- Clean, modular code structure
- Each demo comes with a standalone README: introduces design ideas, implementation details and known drawbacks
- Suitable for studying Linux char device driver development and various kernel subsystems

